# Copy to .env, fill in the values under Required, then uncomment exactly one mode
# block. To unset something, comment the line out: an empty value is not the same as
# an absent one, and several of these reject an empty string.


# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The address browsers use to reach Keycloak, not a bind address: it already listens
# on all interfaces. This and the port together form the issuer in every token, which
# is every user's identity, so changing either one after people have accounts stops
# their logins working. Settle both before the first start. Prefer a name over an IP,
# because a name can be repointed and can later hold a certificate.
#   localhost              local development, browser on the same machine
#   192.168.1.50           reachable by IP on your network
#   auth.home.arpa         a name you resolve yourself (needs your own DNS)
#   auth.example.com       a domain you own
FENNIXS_HOSTNAME=localhost

# Generate each with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Both are written into the database on the first start and never read from here
# again, so editing one later rotates nothing. Keycloak then crash-loops with
# "password authentication failed", while the Postgres superuser silently keeps its
# old password and the stack still reports healthy. To rotate either, change it here
# and then tell the database, which does not ask for the old one:
#   docker compose exec fennixs-postgres \
#     psql -U postgres -c "ALTER ROLE postgres PASSWORD 'the new value'"
#   docker compose exec fennixs-postgres \
#     psql -U postgres -c "ALTER ROLE fennixs_keycloak PASSWORD 'the new value'"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
AUTH_DB_PASSWORD=

# A temporary admin, created on the first start only, so editing these later changes
# nothing. Generate the password like the two above: left empty, Keycloak crash-loops
# on one line about the bootstrap admin username, which names neither this variable
# nor this file. Once the stack is running, sign in, create your own admin account,
# delete this one from inside Keycloak, then remove these two lines. Removing them is
# safe and intended: they do nothing once an admin exists. Until you do, a working
# admin credential sits here in plain text.
KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
KC_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=


# --- Pick one mode, by how TLS reaches your users ---------------------------
# Uncomment exactly one block below. Each sets ENABLE_HTTP, and sets FENNIXS_PORT to
# the port its scheme already owns so that nobody has to type one: change that value
# if something else on this host holds it. Uncomment two and nothing warns you:
# Compose keeps the last value of a repeated key, so the blocks merge into whatever
# hybrid that produces. A certificate alongside ENABLE_HTTP=true is the one combination
# refused outright, with a message naming both.

# Mode 1: plain HTTP, reached over a VPN or a trusted LAN.
# The tunnel provides the encryption. Keycloak already permits plaintext from 10.x,
# 172.16-31.x and 192.168.x, so a WireGuard or OpenVPN tunnel handing out one of those
# needs ENABLE_HTTP only. Tailscale and anything else assigning from 100.64.0.0/10 is
# refused: that case needs FENNIXS_SSL_REQUIRED as well, which is further down under
# "Permanent on the first start", or logins fail with "HTTPS required".
# Do not expose this port to the internet, and note that a host firewall will not stop
# you doing so: see FENNIXS_BIND_ADDRESS below.
#ENABLE_HTTP=true
#FENNIXS_PORT=80

# Mode 2: Keycloak terminates TLS itself.
# FENNIXS_CERTS_DIR is a path, absolute or starting with ./, to a directory that must
# already exist: a bare name such as certs is read as a Docker volume and nothing
# starts at all, Postgres included, and a missing path is created root-owned and
# unreadable. Keycloak runs as uid 1000 and has to traverse that directory and read
# both files. It re-reads them hourly, so a renewed certificate needs no restart. The
# two paths below are inside the container, where only the file names are yours. Using
# a Java keystore instead? Drop them and point KC_HTTPS_KEY_STORE_FILE at a file in
# the same directory.
#ENABLE_HTTP=false
#FENNIXS_PORT=443
#FENNIXS_CERTS_DIR=/srv/fennixs/certs
#KC_HTTPS_CERTIFICATE_FILE=/opt/keycloak/conf/certs/fullchain.pem
#KC_HTTPS_CERTIFICATE_KEY_FILE=/opt/keycloak/conf/certs/privkey.pem
#
# Let's Encrypt: do not point FENNIXS_CERTS_DIR anywhere under /etc/letsencrypt. The
# files in live/ are symlinks into a 0700 directory and the key is 0600 root, so uid
# 1000 cannot read them there and Keycloak reports only that it cannot read the
# certificate. Copy them out on each renewal instead, with a hook saved as
# /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/fennixs.sh and made executable:
#   #!/bin/sh
#   install -o 1000 -g 0 -m 0644 "$RENEWED_LINEAGE/fullchain.pem" /srv/fennixs/certs/
#   install -o 1000 -g 0 -m 0600 "$RENEWED_LINEAGE/privkey.pem"   /srv/fennixs/certs/
# certbot sets RENEWED_LINEAGE itself. Run both lines by hand once before the first
# start, to populate the directory.

# Mode 3: a reverse proxy in front terminates TLS.
# Caddy, nginx, Traefik, a Cloudflare Tunnel. Keycloak speaks plain HTTP on the
# internal network and the proxy holds the certificate. Set FENNIXS_HOSTNAME above to
# the full https:// URL rather than a bare name: that pins the scheme, so session
# cookies carry Secure and the issuer cannot drift if the proxy ever stops sending
# X-Forwarded-Proto. Leave FENNIXS_PORT alone, because the proxy holds 443, and
# FENNIXS_SSL_REQUIRED alone, because the proxy connects from a private address.
#
# KC_PROXY_HEADERS makes Keycloak read X-Forwarded-*. With the https:// hostname above
# the cookies are already Secure without it, but the client address is not: every
# login is recorded against the proxy, and brute-force counting sees one client where
# there are many. Your proxy must SET and OVERWRITE these headers rather than pass on
# whatever the client sent. In nginx that means X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr, not
# $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for, which appends to the client's own value; Caddy and
# Traefik do it correctly on their own. A wrong X-Forwarded-Port lands in every URL
# Keycloak issues.
#
# KC_PROXY_TRUSTED_ADDRESSES is who may assert those headers: one IP, a CIDR, or
# several separated by commas. A hostname is rejected at startup, but a wrong address
# is not, and the headers are then ignored with no error anywhere. Set it to whatever
# your proxy connects from, or anyone who can reach this port may claim any client
# address and the event log will record it as fact.
#ENABLE_HTTP=true
#KC_PROXY_HEADERS=xforwarded
#KC_PROXY_TRUSTED_ADDRESSES=192.168.1.10


# --- Optional: narrow which addresses answer --------------------------------
# FENNIXS_BIND_ADDRESS limits which of this host's addresses the published port
# answers on. Left out, Docker publishes on all of them, IPv4 and IPv6. A host
# firewall will not narrow that: Docker forwards into its own firewalld zone, whose
# policy accepts any ingress zone, and on ufw hosts the packet is redirected before
# ufw's rules are reached, so "ufw deny 8080" looks like it worked and did nothing.
# This is the setting that does work.
#   192.168.1.50   one LAN address
#   10.8.0.1       a VPN interface, so only tunnel clients reach it
#   127.0.0.1      Mode 3 only, and only with the proxy on this same host: people
#                  sign in through a browser, and a browser is never on loopback
# Naming one address stops every other one answering, loopback included, so "curl
# http://localhost:8080" on the host is then refused and that is the setting working
# rather than a fault. An IPv4 address here also stops IPv6 being published.
#FENNIXS_BIND_ADDRESS=192.168.1.50


# --- Permanent on the first start -------------------------------------------
# FENNIXS_SSL_REQUIRED is written into the realm while the realm does not yet exist
# and is never read from here again. Unlike everything above, a wrong value is not
# one restart away from fixed: afterwards it moves only in the admin console, or by
# recreating the database volume and losing every account. It sits outside the mode
# blocks so that picking a mode cannot set it by accident.
#   external   the default, and correct for Modes 2 and 3: plaintext is refused
#              except from a private address
#   none       plaintext accepted from anywhere, which Mode 1 needs only when the
#              tunnel assigns outside 10/8, 172.16/12 and 192.168/16, in practice
#              meaning Tailscale's 100.64.0.0/10
#FENNIXS_SSL_REQUIRED=none


# --- Changed in Keycloak, not here ------------------------------------------
# These three live in the realm, which this file writes only while the realm does not
# yet exist. After the first start the admin console is the only thing that changes
# them, and editing them here does nothing and says nothing:
#   Require SSL         Realm settings > General   (FENNIXS_SSL_REQUIRED, above)
#   Self-registration   Realm settings > Login     (ships closed)
#   SMTP                Realm settings > Email     (password reset, email verification)
